WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.230
- What's up everyone, I'm chef Joe Sasto.

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.720
And today I'm gonna show you

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.500
how I use this 500 year old torchio

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.070
to make a delicious bowl
of pasta, and as a bonus,

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.220
I'm gonna give you a
Madeira cream sauce recipe

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.133
that you can use on anything,
now let's get to it.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.730
I got my bag of tricks here,
so we have here a torchio.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:24.950
This is a solid bronze
piece of machinery used

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.000
to make pasta, 16th century.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.950
This is over hundreds of
years old and how they used

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.480
to make all of those
extruded shapes of pasta

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.277
that you're used to
eating every single day.

00:00:34.277 --> 00:00:36.420
And we also have our handle.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.440
This is going to allow
us to clamp onto the top

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.910
and apply pressure, and our dye,

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:45.660
these come in all
different shapes and sizes

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:46.870
and they're interchangeable.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.550
So depending on which
one you have in there,

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.670
will determine what shape of
pasta that you end up making.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:53.650
I'm excited to show you
how to use this thing.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:55.220
But before we get into that,

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.090
I got to show you how to
make some pasta dough.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:02.940
We have a finely ground
rimacinata semolina durum flour.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.430
We're using all egg yolks for this one.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.770
I have a finely ground
double zero white flour,

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.150
and then I have a coarsely
ground semolina flour.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.380
A little bit of salt,
and then we'll just start

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.180
by doing this all in the stand mixer.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.160
You could do this all by hand,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.620
but I do like taking some
shortcuts when I can.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.400
We're gonna get all of our
flowers into the mixer first,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.180
our durum flour, then
our double zero, finally,

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:31.070
our semolina and a little bit of salt.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.820
We're going to give this a quick mix

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.570
and we can go with all
of our egg yolks at once.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.283
And we're just gonna let this rip.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.890
Now you'll notice this dough
is gonna be a little different

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.600
than some other pasta
doughs you made up there.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.920
It's gonna be harder, a little more dense,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.630
and we're gonna add just
enough water to allow it

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:48.670
to come together.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:52.170
So just a few sprays at a time,
I'm using a very old piece

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.470
of equipment to make the pasta,

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.550
but I'm using a very modern piece

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:56.670
of equipment to make the dough.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.610
I like just combining those
two different worlds together.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.060
We're getting very close here.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.780
We can see that it's gotten
a little more sticky here.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.610
I'm able to squeeze it into my hands

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:07.900
and it holds together.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.230
This is exactly where I want it to be.

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:11.280
We're going to leave it just like this.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.200
And we're gonna bring it
together right on the counter,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.700
squeezing this altogether.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.400
Now, if you didn't have a machine,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.630
this is what the entire
process would look like.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:22.070
And I'm purposely forming
this into a cylinder.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:25.165
So that way it's easier
to load into our machine.

00:02:25.165 --> 00:02:27.730
This hard dough is ready to
go, this just needs to rest.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.980
I like to do that in a vacuum sealed bag.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.540
A lot of pasta chefs look
down on using a vacuum sealer

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.630
with their pasta dough, I do not.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.840
I like taking this extra step here.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.380
It force hydrates the dough

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.700
and speeds up the resting process.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.650
So we're just gonna take our dough log.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.730
We will slide it into
our bag and then right

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.693
into our vacuum machine.

00:02:53.146 --> 00:02:54.540
(machine whirs)

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.373
Like magic.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.870
So we have our dough here,
force hydrating, resting.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:01.670
We're just gonna set this aside to rest

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:05.523
and set up our ancient pasta
gadget, you ready for this?

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.070
So we have all of our pieces
here to our pasta gadget,

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.770
our torchio, starting
with our main frame here.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.030
So what we want to do first is
put on our dye, in this case,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.380
I always like to leave my
dye submerged in water,

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.130
especially when I had used them before.

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.960
So that way the pasta
doesn't dry on the inside.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.300
Today, I'm gonna show
you how to make Gargati.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.950
Think of it almost as
like a thicker spaghetti

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:31.350
or a thinner penne

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:35.080
We'll drop that here into our screw,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.667
attach that to the bottom.

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:39.880
And then we just want to
clamp this onto our table.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.070
Sometimes you need to get a
little creative figuring out how

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:44.730
and where are you're going to clamp it.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.379
I like to use the C
clamps that I have here.

00:03:47.379 --> 00:03:50.173
And we'll just go under the table,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.320
and the other one, all
right, we are secure.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.200
Last thing here, we have our handle.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.680
This'll go right on top
and we can open it up.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.090
And the dough's gonna
go right into that too.

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:09.340
So we have our gadget assembled,

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.173
time to load it with our dough.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:15.980
This has been resting
only about 20, 30 minutes.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.163
Not very long at all.

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:20.570
If you didn't have a vacuum bag,

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.840
you probably want to let this
rest one to two hours or so.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.270
So it really makes a big difference

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.310
and speeds up the entire process.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:31.070
I'm probably going to split
this up into two batches,

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:35.073
down into here, we'll just slide that in.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:39.860
We'll throw this on
top, attach our handle,

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:41.691
and then you just want
to get it started up.

00:04:41.691 --> 00:04:42.524
(imitates engine)

00:04:42.524 --> 00:04:44.330
No, I'm kidding, it's not electric.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:49.047
We're going to go right down,
starting to turn this end.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.683
This is your workout for the day.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.170
So you can tell right,
when you get to the end,

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:57.760
it's not going to turn anymore.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.550
And the moment of truth, you can take all

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:06.550
of your pasta ribbons, bring
them right up, line them up

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.260
and lay them onto your work surface.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.100
Now I just open it back up

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.650
and we can load in our
second piece of dough.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.500
Slide that down.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.000
So what I really like about
this particular shape,

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.960
the gargati, is that there is
actually a small hole forming

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.580
through the center of
each of these noodles.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:31.890
And it has this super unique
mouthfeel when you bite into it

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:33.390
that I absolutely love.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:36.847
All right, so that's done,
look at that, beautiful.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.490
We'll come in with a little
bit of semolina flour here

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.620
so they don't stick together.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.230
Now they have beautiful
texture on the exterior here.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:51.830
That's gonna help grip our sauce,

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.610
provide wonderful mouthfeel.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.310
So we'll allow it to dry
for just a few minutes

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:57.490
before cutting them.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:00.010
And that's going to keep that
hole right through the center

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:04.480
of the pasta, I usually go
about an inch and a half.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.271
I feel like that gives
you real nice texture

00:06:07.271 --> 00:06:12.271
and length all the way across,
not too long, not too short.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:13.610
But at the same time,

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.060
you can cut these into
small little macaroni

00:06:17.987 --> 00:06:22.987
or you can leave them
in really long noodles.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.710
So just depending on how you're feeling,

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:28.270
you can have a whole variety of pasta

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:30.890
just from this one dye, I got a tray here,

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.210
dusted with just a little bit of semolina.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.460
I'm gonna keep them separated.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.423
Now we'll get started on
our Madeira cream sauce.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.880
So we have some cream, we
have thyme, peppercorns,

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.810
some slice shallots and Madeira,

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:49.150
which is a fortified wine and
kind of tastes like Sherry

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:51.410
or port, and then a little
bit of vegetable oil

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:52.243
to get us started.

00:06:52.243 --> 00:06:54.530
So in my sauce pot here
to get things going,

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.393
we're just going to start
heating up our induction burner,

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:02.350
a little bit of vegetable
oil down on the bottom.

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:05.230
Then we'll come in with
our slice shallots.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:06.190
We want a little bit of sweating.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:07.340
We want a little bit of sautéing.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:10.153
So we got a little bit of
action going on here in our pan,

00:07:10.153 --> 00:07:12.380
shallots are sweating down.

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:14.990
We're not really looking
for color here in this case.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.420
I really liked some black peppercorns.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:22.210
And then as much or as
little thyme as you have.

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.300
Today, I have a lot of thyme.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:25.310
You know, if you're short
on thyme, don't be afraid.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.250
You can put a little bit of thyme.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:29.150
We're just going to let that soften.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:31.530
The peppercorns just toast up.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.050
Oh, it starts to get
very fragrant, aromatic.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.693
Our Madeira de glazed with our wine.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.040
And we don't need to worry

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:43.610
about cooking off that
alcohol immediately,

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.830
because we're gonna add our
cream and let that reduce.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:50.830
Stir in the thyme, our
thyme is going to infuse.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.580
You'd always de glaze with
a little bit of lemon juice

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:54.620
works very well.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:58.840
So our sauce base has
reduced by about half here.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:01.883
We'll just come in here and strain this.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.947
We have our infused cream here

00:08:04.947 --> 00:08:07.763
and it's gonna be our sauce
base, let's cook some pasta.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.590
We're gonna go a little bit
of salt in our pasta water.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:13.720
I'm gonna take a moment here,

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:17.070
just to let everyone know your
pasta water should never be

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:18.640
as salty as the sea.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.230
It should taste like water
soup, a well-seasoned stock.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:22.380
We're gonna take all of our pasta now,

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:26.730
go down into our water,
bring this back up to a boil,

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:28.330
throw the lid on for that.

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:31.430
So it's been about two minutes,
do a little taste test here.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.423
Careful it's hot, don't burn yourself.

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:37.390
One color all the way through.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:40.280
You can still see that
hole right in the center.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:41.950
Let's bring these guys out.

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:44.780
I like using a spider,
we'll go into a saute pan,

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:47.510
bringing a little bit of
that pasta water with us.

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:50.130
Noodles back on the heat here.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:54.280
Now we can come in with
our Madeira cream sauce.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.860
So what we want to do
here is marry the noodle

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:58.370
and the sauce together.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:01.520
We're gonna go, a little
bit of butter here.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.433
We just want to emulsify that butter in.

00:09:04.433 --> 00:09:09.170
I always like to come in with
another splash of raw Madeira.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:10.360
So that way you have a little bit

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:12.360
of that cooked off Madeira flavor,

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.260
a little bit of that raw
Madeira flavor coming through,

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:18.080
some fresh cracked black pepper.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.470
And then we're just
gonna swirl this together

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:24.653
to emulsify, we'll see
this start to thicken up,

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:28.360
reach into your pocket and
find yourself a tasting spoon.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:34.040
Don't burn your mouth, love
that, I'm gonna kill the heat.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.420
Let that carry over, have a nice big chunk

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:40.020
of parmigiano Reggiano cheese
I'm going to grate on top.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.430
This is going to add a
nice bit of saltiness.

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:46.320
It's delicious, nice big
spoonful of our pasta.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:48.960
Look at that.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.040
We have some black truffle
we're gonna grade on top.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:53.310
Of course if you don't have truffle

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:54.200
or you're not into truffle,

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:56.770
you can skip this step,
but I happen to love it.

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:00.760
Truffle, Madeira cream,
gargati, baby, my friends.

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:02.740
Look at that, time for a taste test,

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:06.400
reach into your other pocket,
find yourself a golden fork.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.653
Come in, a little bit of everything.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:14.670
Mm, rich, creamy, satisfying.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:16.110
You would call it old reliable.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:18.330
And even if you don't have this gadget,

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:20.940
you could still use the
sauce on any type of pasta

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:21.870
that you have.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:25.820
Don't forget to tag me when
you make it at chef.joe.sasto.

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:26.820
Thanks for watching.

00:10:28.564 --> 00:10:31.147
(upbeat music)

